hadiths[15]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1474","Ja'far bin Muhammad narrates from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mortgaged his armor with a Jew named Abu al-Shahm from Banu Zufar."
"1475","Ja'far bin Muhammad narrates from his father, he said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mortgaged his armor with Abu al-Shahm, the Jew."
"1476","Ja'far bin Muhammad narrates from his father that the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ mortgaged his armor with Abu al-Shahm, the Jew."
"1477","It is narrated from Sa'id bin al-Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The mortgaged item will not be withheld from the one who has mortgaged it; the mortgager has the benefit of the mortgaged item and he will also bear its liability."" Imam Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""'Ghunmah' means its increase and benefit, and 'ghurmah' means its destruction or loss."
"1478","A narration similar or with the same meaning is also reported from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) through Ibn Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him)."
"1479","It is narrated from Sa'id bin al-Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The mortgaged item will not be withheld from the one who has mortgaged it; its benefit is for him, and he is also responsible for its loss."
"1480","Through another chain, this hadith is narrated from the Prophet ﷺ on the authority of Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him), similar to the narration of Ibn Abi Dhi’b’s chain."
"1481","Zaid bin Aslam narrates from his father that the two sons of Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), Abdullah and Ubaidullah (may Allah be pleased with them), set out with the army of the Mujahideen towards Iraq. When both of them turned back, they passed by one of Umar’s (may Allah be pleased with him) governors, who welcomed them, and he was the governor of Basra. He said: “If I had authority over any matter by which I could benefit you, I would certainly do so.” Then he said: “Yes! (I remember) Here is some wealth from the wealth of Allah which I want to send to the Commander of the Faithful. I will give it to you as a loan; you both buy goods from Iraq with it and sell them in Madinah. Then give the principal amount to the Commander of the Faithful, and the profit will be yours.” Both of them said: “We agree, give us the wealth.” Then the governor of Basra wrote a letter to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) for them to receive the wealth. When both of them came to Madinah, they sold the goods and made a profit. Then when they gave the principal amount to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), he asked both of them: “Did he give the same loan to all the people of the army as he gave to both of you?” They said: “No.” So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: “A loan was given only to the two sons of the Commander of the Faithful! You must pay both the principal and the profit obtained from it.” Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) remained silent upon hearing this, but Ubaidullah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: “O Commander of the Faithful! This is not permissible for you, because if the wealth had been destroyed or there had been a loss, we would have been responsible for it (we would have had to bear it).” Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) again said: “Pay it.” Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) remained silent, but Ubaidullah (may Allah be pleased with him) repeated his statement. Then one of the people sitting with Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: “O Commander of the Faithful! If you give it to them in the form of qirad (mudarabah), it would be better.” Then Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) took the principal and half of the profit, and Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) and Ubaidullah (may Allah be pleased with him) took half of the profit from that wealth."
"1482","Hisham bin Urwah narrates from his father, who said: Abdullah bin Ja'far (may Allah be pleased with him) bought something, so Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I will go to Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) and file a case against you to prevent you from disposing (of your wealth)."" When Abdullah bin Ja'far (may Allah be pleased with him) told this to Zubair (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: ""I am your partner in this sale."" Then they went to Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him), and Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Prevent him from disposing (of his wealth), for he is being extravagant."" So Zubair (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I am his partner."" Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""How can I prevent a man from disposing (of his wealth) when Zubair (may Allah be pleased with him) is his partner?"
"1483","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""If a man becomes insolvent, and another man finds his own property with the insolvent person in its original form, then he has more right to it than others."
"1484","Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever finds his own property intact with a bankrupt person, he has more right to it than others."
"1485","The judge of Madinah, Abu Khalda Al-Zurqi, narrated that we brought a destitute man to Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him), so he said: Regarding this, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever dies or becomes destitute, then if the owner of the goods finds his property in its original condition, he has a greater right to it."
"1486","Zayd bin Khalid al-Juhani (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked about a lost item (something found on the road). The Prophet ﷺ said: ""Examine its pouch and its binding thoroughly, then announce it for one year. If its owner comes during that period, give it to him; otherwise, it becomes yours."
"1487","Ubaidullah bin Badr narrated that he traveled on the roads of Syria and found a pouch on the way in which there were eighty dinars. Then he mentioned this to Umar bin Al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), so Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Announce it at the doors of the mosque, and inform everyone who comes from Syria for a year, and when a year has passed, then the pouch is yours."
"1488","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah be pleased with him) that a man picked up something that had fallen on the road, then he came to Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) and said: I have found a lost item, what should I do now? Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: Announce it. He said: I have done so. Ibn Umar said: Do more. He said: I have done a lot. Then Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: I do not permit you to eat such a thing; if you wished, you would not have picked it up."
